<section class="section section--flush">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The honest starting point</h2>

      <p>
        Nobody has a decade of data on optimizing for AI answer surfaces,
        because they haven't existed for a decade. Anyone selling certainty here
        is overselling. What I can offer is a reasoned position, a willingness
        to test, and enough technical grounding to adapt quickly as the evidence
        changes.
      </p>

      <h2>What genuinely changes</h2>

      <h3>Zero-click grows, and the funnel shifts earlier</h3>
      <p>
        When an AI Overview answers the research-stage question directly, the
        informational click you used to earn may not happen. This is most acute
        for exactly the content that performs well in considered-purchase
        verticals: the "what is", "how do I know if", and "what should I ask"
        questions a family researches before they're ready to contact anyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        The strategic implication is not to stop producing that content. It's
        to stop measuring it solely by sessions, and to recognize that being
        <em>cited</em> in the answer is now part of the return.
      </p>

      <h3>Entity clarity matters more than it used to</h3>
      <p>
        Answer engines need to resolve who you are, what you do, and where you
        do it with high confidence. Structured data, consistent NAP, unambiguous
        service descriptions, and clean internal linking were always good
        practice. They're now doing load-bearing work for whether a system will
        confidently name you.
      </p>

      <h3>Extractability becomes a content property</h3>
      <p>
        Content that answers a question in a clearly delimited passage is easier
        to lift into an answer than the same information diffused across eight
        paragraphs of narrative. Clear headings that match real questions,
        direct answers placed early, and genuine specificity all help. So does
        saying something a model cannot synthesize from the other nine results.
      </p>

      <h3>Measurement has to catch up</h3>
      <p>
        Standard reporting doesn't yet cleanly separate AI-surface visibility
        from traditional organic. Any strategist claiming precise AI attribution
        today is overstating. What I'd do is establish a baseline now &mdash;
        tracking brand citation in AI answers for priority queries, monitoring
        impression and CTR divergence as an early signal, and watching the
        research-stage pages where impact will show first.
      </p>

      <h2>What doesn't change</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Crawlability and rendering.</strong> If a system can't reliably
          access and parse your content, none of this matters. AI surfaces
          raised the stakes on technical fundamentals rather than retiring them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Genuine expertise and local specificity.</strong> Generic
          content was already weak. It's now trivially substitutable by a model.
          The defensible asset is information that only you have &mdash; your
          actual locations, staff, pricing reality, and community.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The conversion still happens with you.</strong> An AI answer
          can inform a family's research. It can't tour your community or book
          the appointment. The destination experience still determines whether
          visibility turns into revenue.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Local intent still resolves locally.</strong> Map packs,
          profiles, and proximity signals remain central for "near me" intent.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What I'd do in the first 90 days</h2>

      <ol>
        <li>Baseline AI-surface visibility for priority queries per market, so
          there's something to measure against later.</li>
        <li>Audit entity clarity: structured data coverage and accuracy, NAP
          consistency, and whether the site unambiguously states who and where
          each location is.</li>
        <li>Identify the research-stage content most exposed to zero-click, and
          decide deliberately which pieces to defend, restructure, or retire.</li>
        <li>Improve extractability on the pages worth defending &mdash; question-
          matched headings, direct early answers, FAQ schema where it's
          genuinely warranted.</li>
        <li>Report honestly, separating what we can measure from what we're
          inferring.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Why I'm comfortable here</h2>

      <p>
        I already use AI daily in audit and gap-analysis workflows, which has
        given me a working sense of what these systems do well and where they
        confidently produce nonsense. That's a more useful foundation for
        strategy than reading about it &mdash; and it's the same instinct that
        tells me when to trust an AI-generated finding and when to verify it
        myself.
      </p>
